{"stats":{"took":113,"total":2,"estimate":false,"maxScore":109.52885},"results":[{"_meta":{"score":109.52885},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"OTHER","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20040088701,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"9ecdd85fbfc44d2e894bc7db54453930","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Aerospace Medicine"],"keywords":["Soyuz Project","Non-NASA Center","manned","long duration","short duration","NASA Experiment Number 9401628","STS Shuttle Project","NASA Discipline Space Human Factors","Flight Experiment","Mir Project","Astronauts/psychology","Space Flight","Adaptation, Psychological","Weightlessness","Interpersonal Relations","Questionnaires","Aerospace Medicine","Regression Analysis","Comparative Study","Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S","United States","Models, Psychological","Human","Russia","Social Isolation/psychology","Affect","Group Processes"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20040088701,"ear":"NO","id":"2ea33ab659124dda842350d694705ffb","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2011-08-23T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","otherReportNumbers":["ISSN: 0094-5765"],"title":"Human interactions in space: results from Shuttle/Mir","stiType":"REPRINT","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2013-08-21T20:06:00.0000000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20040088701,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Kanas, N."},"organization":{"name":"and the VA Medical Center","location":"4150 Clement Street, San Francisco, California 94121, United States"}},"id":"bfe04155a5844618a4e2900587c7447d"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":20040088701,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Salnitskiy, V."},"organization":{}},"id":"2ad0d8a7ef0e45ca9eb2cf21bd786642"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":20040088701,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Grund, E. M."},"organization":{}},"id":"39e9d15655014d37b642a9512c3184bd"},{"sequence":3,"submissionId":20040088701,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Weiss, D. S."},"organization":{}},"id":"6b7c0dd64ff94f8d835ce12d9b5a21e4"},{"sequence":4,"submissionId":20040088701,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Gushin, V."},"organization":{}},"id":"03b10a0c232545b4b267fdc6b1876c2a"},{"sequence":5,"submissionId":20040088701,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Kozerenko, O."},"organization":{}},"id":"2986a846c29e4992b22787c47ff85784"},{"sequence":6,"submissionId":20040088701,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Sled, A."},"organization":{}},"id":"4e6445dec8a04880affefe100069fc28"},{"sequence":7,"submissionId":20040088701,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Marmar, C. R."},"organization":{}},"id":"cf3a159009c9463f8feb93becde816ac"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Reprint (Version printed in journal)","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20040088701,"sourceIdentifiers":[{"number":"doi:10.1016/S0094-5765(01)00103-5","submissionId":20040088701,"id":"8cbd07a784704c9b9b44fc4800816431","type":"DOI"},{"submissionId":20040088701,"id":"f9f1bd49df8347d4a9688cb63dbc865e","descriptor":"doi:10.1016/S0094-5765(01)00103-5"}],"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2013-08-21T20:06:00.0000000+00:00","center":{"code":"CDMS","name":"Legacy CDMS","id":"092d6e0881874968859b972d39a888dc"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Background: Anecdotal reports from space and results from simulation studies on Earth have suggested that space crewmembers may experience decrements in their interpersonal environment over time and may displace tension and dysphoria to mission control personnel. Methods: To evaluate these issues, we studied 5 American astronauts, 8 Russian cosmonauts, and 42 American and 16 Russian mission control personnel who participated in the Shuttle/Mir space program. Subjects completed questions from subscales of the Profile of Mood States, the Group Environment Scale, and the Work Environment Scale on a weekly basis before, during, and after the missions. Results: Among the crewmembers, there was little evidence for significant time effects based on triphasic (U-shaped) or linear models for the 21 subscales tested, although the presence of an initial novelty effect that declined over time was found in three subscales for the astronauts. Compared with work groups on Earth, the crewmembers reported less dysphoria and perceived their crew environment as more constraining, cohesive, and guided by leadership. There was no change in ratings of mood and interpersonal environment before, during, and after the missions. Conclusions: There was little support for the presence of a moderate to strong time effect that influenced the space crews. Crewmembers perceived their work environment differently from people on Earth, and they demonstrated equanimity in mood and group perceptions, both in space and on the ground. Grant numbers: NAS9-19411. c 2001. Elsevier Science Ltd. All rights reserved.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"METADATA_ONLY","publications":[{"volume":"49","submissionId":20040088701,"issue":"3-10","issn":"0094-5765","id":"becec2ec490c48e4aa0cfcefd0bd09a2","publicationName":"Acta astronautica","publicationDate":"2001-08-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[],"downloadsAvailable":false,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":97.27839},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"OTHER","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20040088445,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"0ad319aeaa354ebab702b82f1f55d540","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Life Sciences (General)"],"keywords":["Non-NASA Center","long duration","NASA Experiment Number 9401628","manned","Flight Experiment","short duration","NASA Discipline Space Human Factors","Mir Project","STS Shuttle Project","Astronauts/psychology","Space Flight","Weightlessness","Displacement (Psychology)","Interpersonal Relations","Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S","Human","United States","Stress, Psychological","Group Processes","Adaptation, Psychological","Cross-Cultural Comparison","Affect","Russia","Questionnaires"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20040088445,"ear":"NO","id":"e97e32837e25487fbb7135c8ee1b4c34","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2011-08-23T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","otherReportNumbers":["ISSN: 0094-5765"],"title":"Human interactions during Shuttle/Mir space missions","stiType":"REPRINT","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2013-08-21T20:05:00.0000000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20040088445,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Kanas, N."},"organization":{"name":"University of California and Department of Veterans Affairs Medical Center","location":"San Francisco, California, United States"}},"id":"58b27549ff77486a98f1c3c172abe07d"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":20040088445,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Salnitskiy, V."},"organization":{}},"id":"67fb6e7188f141e98a102b56f5baebbf"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":20040088445,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Grund, E. M."},"organization":{}},"id":"073e051406f34ad8be3b6af0aed9f4be"},{"sequence":3,"submissionId":20040088445,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Weiss, D. S."},"organization":{}},"id":"18bbac5922994729a6497d3b1aaf40de"},{"sequence":4,"submissionId":20040088445,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Gushin, V."},"organization":{}},"id":"8b5ca4b4b0654af4b0afb0406bf0b9de"},{"sequence":5,"submissionId":20040088445,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Kozerenko, O."},"organization":{}},"id":"8e425614e5f942538cb1f62a4c764cc4"},{"sequence":6,"submissionId":20040088445,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Sled, A."},"organization":{}},"id":"46bb51961f3043f092f721e8cdbc4898"},{"sequence":7,"submissionId":20040088445,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Marmar, C. R."},"organization":{}},"id":"aaf1dc6f2624495391374541411624ed"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Reprint (Version printed in journal)","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20040088445,"sourceIdentifiers":[{"number":"doi:10.1016/S0094-5765(01)00024-8","submissionId":20040088445,"id":"60b3d644983d4c92a0ba48a7f5d0d462","type":"DOI"},{"submissionId":20040088445,"id":"f4631f79052f4b6bbbc4514b0939c0a8","descriptor":"doi:10.1016/S0094-5765(01)00024-8"}],"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2013-08-21T20:05:00.0000000+00:00","center":{"code":"CDMS","name":"Legacy CDMS","id":"092d6e0881874968859b972d39a888dc"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"To improve the interpersonal climate of crewmembers involved with long-duration space missions, it is important to understand the factors affecting their interactions with each other and with members of mission control. This paper will present findings from a recently completed NASA-funded study during the Shuttle/Mir program which evaluated in-group/out-group displacement of negative emotions; changes in tension, cohesion, and leader support over time; and cultural differences. In-flight data were collected from 5 astronauts, 8 cosmonauts, and 42 American and 16 Russian mission control personnel who signed informed consent. Subjects completed a weekly questionnaire that assessed their mood and perception of their work group's interpersonal climate using questions from well-known, standardized measures (Profile of Mood States, Group and Work Environment Scales) and a critical incident log. There was strong evidence for the displacement of tension and dysphoric emotions from crewmembers to mission control personnel and from mission control personnel to management. There was a perceived decrease in commander support during the 2nd half of the missions, and for American crewmembers a novelty effect was found on several subscales during the first few months on-orbit. There were a number of differences between American and Russian responses which suggested that the former were less happy with their interpersonal environment than the latter. Mission control personnel reported more tension and dysphoria than crewmembers, although both groups scored better than other work groups on Earth. Nearly all reported critical incidents came from ground subjects, with Americans and Russians showing important differences in response frequencies.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"METADATA_ONLY","publications":[{"volume":"48","submissionId":20040088445,"issue":"5-12","issn":"0094-5765","id":"7cf21c76924e4e03a59bb8a4d52e6d5d","publicationName":"Acta astronautica","publicationDate":"2001-03-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[],"downloadsAvailable":false,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"}],"aggregations":{"created":{"buckets":[{"key_as_string":"2013","key":1356998400000,"doc_count":2}]},"author":{"doc_count_error_upper_bound":0,"sum_other_doc_count":0,"buckets":[{"key":"Grund, E. M.","doc_count":2},{"key":"Gushin, V.","doc_count":2},{"key":"Kanas, N.","doc_count":2},{"key":"Kozerenko, O.","doc_count":2},{"key":"Marmar, C. R.","doc_count":2},{"key":"Salnitskiy, V.","doc_count":2},{"key":"Sled, A.","doc_count":2},{"key":"Weiss, D. S.","doc_count":2}]},"reportNumber":{"doc_count_error_upper_bound":0,"sum_other_doc_count":0,"buckets":[{"key":"ISSN: 0094-5765","doc_count":2}]},"center":{"doc_count_error_upper_bound":0,"sum_other_doc_count":0,"buckets":[{"key":"CDMS","doc_count":2}]},"index":{"doc_count_error_upper_bound":0,"sum_other_doc_count":0,"buckets":[{"key":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24","doc_count":2}]},"fundingNumber":{"doc_count_error_upper_bound":0,"sum_other_doc